body:
@valpackett 
Good catch — that is almost certainly it. I was on Ubuntu's 7.0.0-29-generic at the time, which is a stubble-bundled kernel, and stubble replaces a loader-supplied device tree when the compatible strings match. So the finding isn't "the bootloaders can't pass a DTB" but "Ubuntu's stub overrides it". I'll verify with stubble.dtb_override=false plus a devicetree line in GRUB and correct the writeup. Thanks — that would have saved me two days.

@Doloro1978 
Suspend/resume works here — no black screen, no lockup. One caveat on the wake side: I wake it with the power button. That's the trigger I use and it comes back reliably every time; I haven't relied on lid-open or a keypress.

I'm on the ELLX kernel, not Ubuntu's. Worth knowing that the ELLX package tree also ships small display-wake and trackpad-wake fix scripts — if you're getting a black screen on wake, those may be exactly what's missing on your side. Which kernel are you running?

**Surface Laptop 7 touchscreen: static DT node on the ELLX tree — overlay module retired**

Follow-up to the touchpad work here. The touchscreen now comes up from a statically patched device tree, no runtime overlay module involved. Patch is purely additive, 88 lines.

Also: my earlier "a bootloader-supplied DTB never reaches the kernel" was wrong — it was stubble, exactly as @valpackett said in this thread. On ELLX (no stubble) a plain GRUB `devicetree` directive works fine.

```dts
&gpi_dma1 {
	status = "okay";
};

&spi10 {
	compatible = "qcom,geni-spi-qspi";
	status = "okay";
	spi-max-frequency = <40000000>;
	qcom,qspi-read-opcode = <0xeb>;
	qcom,qspi-read-dummy-clocks = <8>;
	qcom,qspi-read-cmd-bytes = <4>;

	dmas = <&gpi_dma1 0 2 QCOM_GPI_QSPI>,
	       <&gpi_dma1 1 2 QCOM_GPI_QSPI>;
	dma-names = "tx", "rx";

	pinctrl-0 = <&qup_spi10_data_clk>, <&qup_spi10_cs>,
		    <&qup_qspi10_data23>, <&spi10_hid0_int_bias>;
	pinctrl-names = "default";

	touchscreen@0 {
		compatible = "hid-over-spi";
		reg = <0>;
		spi-max-frequency = <40000000>;

		interrupt-parent = <&tlmm>;
		interrupts = <51 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_FALLING>;

		vdd-supply = <&vreg_ts2_5p0>;

		pinctrl-0 = <&spi10_hid0_reset_deassert>;
		pinctrl-1 = <&spi10_hid0_reset_assert>;
		pinctrl-names = "active", "reset";

		input-report-header-address = <0x1000>;
		input-report-body-address = <0x1004>;
		output-report-address = <0x2000>;
		read-opcode = <0xeb>;
		write-opcode = <0xe2>;
	};
};
```

Plus four TLMM states (`qup_qspi10_data23` on gpio49/50 as `qup1_se2`; `spi10_hid0_int_bias` on gpio51; `spi10_hid0_reset_deassert`/`_assert` on gpio64+gpio48) and a fixed 5 V regulator on `<&tlmm 64 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>`, `startup-delay-us = <100000>`.

Values come from the SSDT device `GTCH` (`_CID PNP0C51`) on `\_SB.SP11` = `QUP_1_SE_2`, base `0xA88000`. One thing that is easy to get wrong: **GPIO 64 is the supply, not the reset** — the reset is GPIO 48 and never appears in `_CRS` at all, because the firmware drives it by MMIO. It shows up in `GTCH`'s `_INI`/`_PS0`/`_RST` as OperationRegions `GI64` and `GI48`, the same naming scheme as the touchpad's `GI65`/`G120`. Full derivation, and the rest of the SL7 bring-up: https://horizontblau.de/linux/surface-laptop-7-linux.html

Verified by building the unmodified ELLX tree at the exact commit of the running kernel and confirming it reproduces the shipped DTB byte for byte first — with the kernel's own config, not defconfig, since `CONFIG_OF_OVERLAY=y` adds `-@` and the shipped DTB has `__symbols__`. Then booted on an ordinary boot entry, not just a test one.

**Two traps that cost me time:**

1. The `dmas` protocol cell must be `QCOM_GPI_QSPI` (4), not `QCOM_GPI_SPI` (1). With the wrong one the controller probes fine and transfers nothing.

2. Unrelated to DT but relevant if you run `iptsd`: touchpad and touchscreen hidraw numbers are **not** stable across boots. Mine was `/dev/hidraw1` for weeks, then flipped — the two registrations are ~50 ms apart. A unit wired to a fixed number will one day attach to the touchscreen, and the dead touchpad looks exactly like the touch-controller wedge this machine also has. Resolve by bus path (`88c000.spi`) instead.

Deliberate asymmetry, so it doesn't look like an oversight: `spi10` reuses the existing `qup_spi10_*` data-clk/cs states and only adds the two extra data lines — that is what was tested, I didn't harmonise it with the touchpad afterwards.

Happy to send this as a proper patch.

@horizontblau please stop. You cannot just pump out answers from Cladue pretending like a) they're your own and b) they are correct and/or provide good solutions (which they often do not). This is irresponsible. Most of your writeup and "work" have already been investigated by others, and touchpad device tree support has been public on my GitHub profile for ages yet you decided to do your own thing that doesn't remotely make sense. Even though you state it is not your original work, much of it is original work, or at best a so terrible a coupling of the people you've sourced it from that it barely resembles theirs anymore. Everything you've "done" is full of AI-isms which infect your "work" (I use quotations again because you haven't really done much here except act as an interface for the AI into the physical world) with mistakes and misconceptions that stem from an LLM's inability to bigger pictures. What you are doing is completely against the ideals of open-source software, and at the end of the day they will not be helpful in creating a coherent experience on this device. To everyone else lurking here, I'm sorry I never got around to making stuff better; I ended up getting rid of the damn thing as I had to start doing real work and needed something reliable. I can't remember all I did, and it really isn't much if you look at my GitHub, but I did patch up iptsd to work better as that other fork actually missed the obvious solution. And I did get touchscreen to work a few months back, along with I think getting some stuff to resume better. I never got to the bottom of all the resume issues though. Those are going to be much deeper kernel issues than I have the knowledge to support.

I've been watching you interact here silently for a while. You have issued far too many "corrections" to your overstatements and mistakes that you are beginning to lead people on. You simply don't understand a word of what you are talking about. You shouldn't have to correct yourself 100 times because you were provably wrong. If you really want to make a difference in this project or open-source software as a whole, you simply need to learn how the kernel works and how to do shit yourself.

To be completely clear to everybody, and I'd be willing to provide support where I can, the real solution to making touchscreen work is looking at what I've done. I'm sure newer versions of Linux might have a slightly different way about this and that new patches are out, but look at my [NixOS flake](https://github.com/orvitpng/nix1e) and see how those device trees are done and how the kernel modules are built. I do believe that, soon enough, everything will be merged in so device trees will be the only thing needed on mainline. @horizontblau has provided device trees which, while they may work, are riddled with misconceptions about what the options do and are not equivalent to how Windows manages it. I'm not sure if anyone else has commented on this, but I also got close to or did fix the mousepad not waking up properly. I'm not sure if I committed that, and if I didn't, that is lost (but it shouldn't be too far off the trail from what was pushed to GitHub).
